^act1
^transition
FADE IN:
^transition/

^scene
^number
1
^number/
^slugline
EXT. CORMORANT ROCK - DUSK
^slugline/

^action
A fist of black stone a mile off the coast. On it, a white tower, and nothing else. Gulls wheel below the gallery rail. The sea is flat and the color of slate.
^action/

^super
Cormorant Rock. The last manned light on the coast.
^super/

^action
A supply boat pulls away from the landing, leaving one figure on the steps with a duffel bag: TOMAS REY, 19, town shoes, already seasick. He watches the boat go. Then he looks up at the tower, all hundred and ten feet of it.
^action/

^shot
THE LAMP ROOM WINDOWS
^shot/

^action
catch the last of the sun and throw it back, gold, then go dark as the sun drops.
^action/
^scene/

^scene
^number
2
^number/
^slugline
INT. LIGHTHOUSE - WATCH ROOM - CONTINUOUS
^slugline/

^action
A round room. A stove, a radio set older than Tomas, a logbook the size of a paving stone. ADA MARSH, 60s, forty years on this rock, does not look up from the log when he comes in.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^lines
You're the relief.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^dialogue
^character
TOMAS
^character/
^lines
Tomas. Tomas Rey. They said you'd show me the round.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^lines
They say a great deal. Boots by the stove. You'll want them dry by midnight.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^note
Ada never asks him a question in this scene. Keep it that way through the next draft.
^note/

^action
He sits to pull off his shoes. She finally looks at him, and at the shoes, and almost smiles.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
TOMAS
^character/
^lines
How long have you kept it?
^lines/
^dialogue/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^lines
Forty years in March.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^dialogue
^character
TOMAS
^character/
^lines
And they're really closing it.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^parenthetical
(flat)
^parenthetical/
^lines
They are _automating_ it. Which is closing it, with a press release.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
The radio crackles. Both of them turn toward it and speak at once.
^action/

^dual_dialogue
^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^lines
That's the harbor master. Leave it.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^dialogue
^character
TOMAS
^character/
^lines
Should I get that? I can get that.
^lines/
^dialogue/
^dual_dialogue/

^action
He leaves it. The crackle dies.
^action/

^montage
^heading
MONTAGE - ADA TEACHES THE ROUND
^heading/

^shot
Ada's hand on a brass oil can, Tomas's hand copying it a beat late.
^shot/

^shot
The spiral stair, from above. Two lanterns climbing.
^shot/

^action
In the lamp room she shows him the great lens: a cage of glass taller than both of them, floating on its bath of mercury. She turns it with one finger.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^extension
(V.O.)
^extension/
^lines
One finger. If it takes two, something's wrong and you call me.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^shot
A cloth, a prism, a thumbprint wiped away.
^shot/

^super
9:40 PM
^super/

^shot
The lamp lit. The beam swings out over the water for the first time tonight.
^shot/

^note
Six shots is plenty. If this runs long, cut the oil can.
^note/

^closing
END MONTAGE
^closing/
^montage/

^action
Tomas writes the lighting time in the log. His handwriting, under forty years of hers.
^action/
^scene/
^act1/

^omitted
^number
3
^number/
^omitted/

^act2
^sequence
^label
The storm
^label/

^note
Everything in this sequence happens between ten and midnight.
^note/

^scene
^number
4
^number/
^slugline
EXT. OPEN WATER - NIGHT
^slugline/

^action
Rain, sideways. A forty-foot trawler, the MARGUERITE, wallows beam-on to a rising sea. No running lights. No engine note. Just the slap of water on a dead hull.
^action/
^scene/

^intercut
^heading
INTERCUT - WATCH ROOM / MARGUERITE WHEELHOUSE
^heading/

^scene
^number
5
^number/
^slugline
INT. MARGUERITE - WHEELHOUSE - NIGHT
^slugline/

^action
JUNE OKAFOR, 40s, soaked, braced against the wheel with a handset in her fist. One battery lamp. Water moves across the deck plates.
^action/
^scene/

^dialogue
^character
JUNE
^character/
^extension
(INTO RADIO)
^extension/
^lines
Cormorant Light, Cormorant Light, this is Marguerite. I have no engine and no chart plotter. Tell me you can hear me.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
In the watch room Ada is already at the set. Tomas stands behind her with one boot on.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^lines
I hear you, June. Where's your anchor?
^lines/
^dialogue/

^dialogue
^character
JUNE
^character/
^extension
(OVER RADIO)
^extension/
^lines
Dragging. I can see your beam. I can't tell how far.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^lines
Then keep seeing it. You hold that light on your port bow and you'll clear the Teeth. Lose it and you won't.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
June wipes her eyes with the back of her wrist. She is very tired.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
JUNE
^character/
^extension
(OVER RADIO)
^extension/
^lines
Talk to me, Ada. I keep closing my eyes.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
Ada looks at the handset. Then, to Tomas's astonishment, she sings. Not well.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^parenthetical
(singing)
^parenthetical/
^lyrics
Hold to the light, hold to the light,
the rock is black but the lamp is bright,
and morning comes to the ones who wait,
hold to the light, it's not too late.
^lyrics/
^dialogue/

^dialogue
^character
JUNE
^character/
^extension
(OVER RADIO)
^extension/
^parenthetical
(joining, cracked)
^parenthetical/
^lyrics
Hold to the light, it's not too late.
^lyrics/
^dialogue/

^note
The song is original to this example. Ada's mother sang it; that goes unsaid.
^note/

^closing
END INTERCUT
^closing/
^intercut/

^scene
^number
6
^number/
^slugline
INT. LIGHTHOUSE - WATCH ROOM - CONTINUOUS
^slugline/

^action
The stove light flickers. The radio's dial goes dim. Somewhere below, a generator coughs twice and stops.
^action/

^action
Ada and Tomas look up at the ceiling at the same instant.
^action/

^shot
THE BEAM
^shot/

^action
sweeping across the rain, slowing, slowing. It stops. It points at nothing.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
TOMAS
^character/
^lines
It's still lit.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^lines
Lit isn't enough. A light that doesn't turn is a streetlamp. She'll steer for it and it'll put her on the Teeth.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
She is already on the stair. He is one boot behind her.
^action/
^scene/
^sequence/
^act2/

^act3
^scene
^number
7
^number/
^slugline
INT. LIGHTHOUSE - LAMP ROOM - NIGHT
^slugline/

^action
The lens hangs dead in its cage, the lamp blazing inside it. Rain hammers the glass. Ada throws open a locker and hauls out a hand crank the length of her arm.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^lines
One turn every ten seconds. Not nine. Not eleven. Boats count it.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
She fits the crank and leans on it. The lens does not move. She leans harder. Forty years, and tonight her shoulder will not do it.
^action/

^action
Tomas puts his hands beside hers.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
TOMAS
^character/
^lines
Count for me.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^parenthetical
(after a moment)
^parenthetical/
^lines
One. Two. Three.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
The lens shudders, and turns. The beam swings out over the water.
^action/

^shot
TOMAS'S HANDS on the crank. ADA'S FINGER tapping the count on the brass.
^shot/

^dialogue
^character
JUNE
^character/
^extension
(V.O.)
^extension/
^lines
I've got you. I've got you, Cormorant, you're turning. Port bow. I've got you.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
Neither of them answers. There is no breath to spare. They turn the light.
^action/

^title_card
Six hours later
^title_card/
^scene/

^scene
^number
8
^number/
^slugline
EXT. HARBOR MOUTH - DAWN
^slugline/

^action
Gray water going pink. The Marguerite comes in under tow, low in the stern, every soul on the quay watching her.
^action/

^action
On the far horizon the lighthouse is a white thread. Its beam still turns, pale now against the daylight, ten seconds to the sweep.
^action/
^scene/

^scene
^number
9
^number/
^slugline
INT. LIGHTHOUSE - WATCH ROOM - DAY
^slugline/

^action
Tomas asleep in the chair, hands wrapped in rags. Ada opens the log. She finds last night's line, in his handwriting, and under it she writes one word. We do not see the word.
^action/

^action
She caps the pen and sets it on his side of the desk.
^action/

^dialogue
^character
ADA
^character/
^parenthetical
(quietly)
^parenthetical/
^lines
One finger. You call me if it takes two.
^lines/
^dialogue/

^action
She goes to put the kettle on.
^action/
^scene/

^transition
FADE OUT.
^transition/

^title_card
THE END
^title_card/
